---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="An F-16 seen head-on in level flight against a dust-coloured sky, wingtip missiles and underwing stores visible, the pilot's helmet just showing under the canopy"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    The subject is a single-seat, digitally fly-by-wire jet aircraft with no
    instructor seat: the course exists because a pilot's first flight in this
    type is necessarily solo, and everything taught before it is preparation
    for that fact. Teaching runs across twelve weeks in three phases — ground
    instruction, simulator, and live flying — ending in a solo check ride
    assessed on whether the aircraft returns able to fly again.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning outcomes</h2>
  <p>At the completion of this course, you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      perform this aircraft's emergency egress procedure under time pressure
      and without visual reference
    </li>
    <li>
      explain how a relaxed-stability airframe's flight control system
      produces both its handling qualities and its operating limits
    </li>
    <li>
      apply specific energy management to plan and fly an approach that
      arrives at a chosen point in a chosen energy state
    </li>
    <li>
      fly a standard circuit and approach to published tolerances, referenced
      to angle of attack rather than airspeed
    </li>
    <li>
      state, defend and apply a personal decision altitude and approach
      criteria under check-ride conditions
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the course runs</h2>
  <p>
    SLOP4386 runs in Semester 1, 2027, as a Slop University School of Invented
    Disciplines course at undergraduate level 4.
  </p>
  <p>
    Teaching is organised into three phases across twelve weeks: four weeks of
    ground instruction, four weeks in the simulator, and four weeks of live
    flying, the last of these built around a single solo sortie and a check
    ride. Ground and simulator weeks each pair a lecture with a practical
    period; flying weeks have none.
  </p>
  <p>
    The course has three staff: a convenor who teaches the ground phase and
    signs the flying authorisation, a simulator instructor who runs weeks
    5–8, and a crew chief who owns the aircraft and is the only person who can
    declare it unserviceable.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <p>
    Final-year students who have completed a first course in aerodynamics and
    can manipulate an energy equation without derivation. No flying experience
    is assumed or required.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessment</h2>
  <p>
    Assessment is weighted across four components: an egress and systems
    examination (15%), a simulator progression check (20%), a written
    judgement on personal decision-making (15%), and a check ride (50%)
    assessed holistically on whether the aircraft returns able to fly again,
    and whether that outcome followed from the pilot's decisions rather than
    from chance.
  </p>
  <p>
    A go-around initiated on a student's own pre-briefed criteria is not
    penalised in any assessment; continuing an approach past those criteria is
    treated as the failure, independent of how the landing turns out.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, and what happens in each one.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Standards" href="/standards/">
      <p>Every number you are held to, on one page.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Two gates, one written judgement, and the check ride.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this, and who owns the aircraft.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin-block-end: 1rem;
  }
</style>
